                "text": "**Analyzing Red Pixel Behavior**\n\nI'm focusing on the red pixels. In train 1, I observed two red pixels originating from the bottom row at columns 2 and 5, and another at column 10. The output's bottom row shows a different pattern: [5,0,2,0,0,2,0. I am trying to understand this discrepancy.\n\n**Tracing Red Pixel Movement**\n\nMy analysis has shifted. I'm now tracing the precise paths of the red pixels. It appears they move upwards, and I'm investigating how they interact with obstacles (the '5' values), specifically how they change direction when encountering them directly above. This seems to be the key to the observed output pattern.\n\n**Investigating Pixel Deflection**\n\nI'm now scrutinizing the behavior of a red pixel originating at column 5. I've discovered that when this pixel encounters obstacles at row 9 (columns 4 and 6), it becomes trapped and terminates its path. This explains its persistence at column 5 in the output, rather than deflecting.\n\n**Explaining Column Eleven Trajectory**\n\nI've pinpointed the reason for the pixel moving to column 11. It's a sequence of attempts to move upwards. When blocked, it attempts to move right. This rightward movement was triggered by obstacles at its intended upward path. It then successfully moved upwards from column 11.\n\n**Exploring Ray Deflection Mechanics**\n\nI'm now focused on how rays react to obstacles. It appears my previous observation about left turns was incorrect. All instances thus far demonstrate a rightward turn when blocked from moving upwards. This seems to be a consistent rule governing ray behavior when encountering a '5'.\n\n**Testing Ray Deflection Theory**\n\nI'm verifying if rays consistently turn right when blocked. My current observations in train 3 suggest this is indeed the case. When a ray at (9,3) encountered an obstacle at (8,3), it moved right to (9,4) and then (9,5), before successfully ascending from (8,5).\n\n**Refining Ray Obstacle Interaction**\n\nI'm re-evaluating how rays react to obstacles. It seems my assumption about consistent right turns might be flawed. I'm seeing instances where a ray's path is blocked, and it attempts a rightward movement, but the subsequent path then deviates unexpectedly based on further obstacles.\n\n**Observing Continuous Rightward Deflection**\n\nI've identified a critical pattern: all deflections are exclusively to the right. I'm now investigating scenarios where the immediate right cell is also blocked, and how the ray navigates multiple consecutive rightward steps until it finds an open path upwards.\n\n**Testing Ray Path Algorithm**\n\nI'm now formalizing the algorithm based on observations. It seems rays prioritize moving upwards. If blocked, they attempt to move rightward sequentially until an upward path is found. I'm verifying if '2' values also act as obstacles or if rays can pass through or merge with them.\n\n**Tracing Ray Path Algorithm**\n\nI'm now simulating individual ray paths with the refined algorithm. My focus is on how each ray navigates upwards, attempting rightward deflections when encountering '5's. I'm meticulously tracking their positions and marking the grid with '2's to visualize their routes and verify the deflection logic.\n\n**Simulating Ray Deflections in Test Input**\n\nI'm now applying the ray path algorithm to the provided test input. My current trace begins with a ray originating at (12,3). It successfully ascends until row 10, where it encounters a '5' at (9,3). It then attempts a rightward move to (10,4) and finds an upward path from there.\n\n**Verifying Obstacle Interactions**\n\nMy simulation continues, and I'm observing the ray at (10,4) successfully ascending to (9,4). I will now proceed to trace its path further up the grid, paying close attention to any further interactions with obstacles and verifying if my current understanding of ray behavior accurately predicts its trajectory.\n\n**Simulating Ray 2 Deflection**\n\nI'm now tracing